1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,539
This is STAT 331, video 17 a. The topic is the hat matrix, the mean response, and prediction in multiple linear regression.

2
00:00:10,989 --> 00:00:17,431
This video prepares you for two question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:17,881 --> 00:00:26,415
Type A: find the mean and the variance of the fitted values, the residuals, an estimated mean response, and a prediction.

4
00:00:26,865 --> 00:00:33,777
Type B: build a confidence interval for a mean response, and a prediction interval for a new response.

5
00:00:34,227 --> 00:00:44,573
Video 17 b uses these results for three more types: questions on regression output, and practice questions 2 and 3 of Lecture 8.

6
00:00:45,023 --> 00:00:48,949
Start with the model. y is a vector of n responses.

7
00:00:49,399 --> 00:01:00,535
X is the design matrix, with n rows and p plus 1 columns. Its first column is all ones. Beta is the vector of p plus 1 coefficients.

8
00:01:00,985 --> 00:01:10,499
Epsilon is the vector of n errors. It is multivariate normal, with mean 0 and variance sigma squared times the identity.

9
00:01:10,949 --> 00:01:18,245
The least squares estimator is beta hat equals X transpose X inverse, times X transpose y.

10
00:01:18,695 --> 00:01:34,717
From the last videos: beta hat is multivariate normal, with mean beta and variance sigma squared times X transpose X inverse. And y is multivariate normal, with mean X beta and variance sigma squared I.

11
00:01:35,167 --> 00:01:46,217
This video answers two questions. What are the mean and the variance of the fitted values and the residuals? And how sure is an estimate at new covariate values?

12
00:01:46,667 --> 00:01:53,579
The fitted values are y hat equals X times beta hat. This is line 1 of slide 24.

13
00:01:54,029 --> 00:02:03,267
Put in the formula for beta hat: X transpose X inverse, times X transpose y. The picture shows the three matrices.

14
00:02:03,717 --> 00:02:11,418
Move the brackets. A product of matrices can be grouped in any way. So y hat is one matrix times y.

15
00:02:11,868 --> 00:02:24,369
This matrix is X, times X transpose X inverse, times X transpose. Its size is n by n. We call it H, the hat matrix, because it puts the hat on y.

16
00:02:24,819 --> 00:02:32,286
The residuals are e equals y minus y hat. Put in line 4: e equals y minus H y.

17
00:02:32,736 --> 00:02:40,352
Take y out as a common factor. y equals I times y, so e equals I minus H, times y.

18
00:02:40,802 --> 00:02:52,066
So y splits into two parts: H y, the fitted part, and I minus H times y, the residual part. Type A uses these two forms in step 1.

19
00:02:52,516 --> 00:03:07,343
This picture is optional. It is not on the slides, but it shows what H does. Think of y as one point in n dimensions. The purple plane is every vector X times b. It is the column space of X.

20
00:03:07,793 --> 00:03:12,145
y is the blue vector. In general, y is not in the plane.

21
00:03:12,595 --> 00:03:26,227
y hat equals X beta hat, so y hat is in the plane. Least squares picks the point of the plane that is nearest to y. H y is that point. So H projects y onto the plane.

22
00:03:26,677 --> 00:03:37,237
The residual e goes from y hat to y. It is perpendicular to the plane. These are the normal equations: X transpose e equals 0.

23
00:03:37,687 --> 00:03:48,396
A vector in the plane does not change when we project it. So H times y hat equals y hat. The next slides prove the facts about H with algebra.

24
00:03:48,846 --> 00:03:59,257
Fact 1 on slide 25: H is symmetric. Pause the video and try it. Start with H transpose. Write out the definition of H.

25
00:03:59,707 --> 00:04:05,061
The transpose of a product is the product of the transposes, in reverse order.

26
00:04:05,511 --> 00:04:12,829
The transpose of X transpose is X. The transpose of an inverse is the inverse of the transpose.

27
00:04:13,279 --> 00:04:18,761
X transpose X is symmetric: its transpose is X transpose X again.

28
00:04:19,211 --> 00:04:25,718
So H transpose equals X, X transpose X inverse, X transpose. That is H.

29
00:04:26,168 --> 00:04:36,899
Check it on a small example, not from the slides. X has a column of ones and x equals 1, 2, 3, 4. This is its 4 by 4 hat matrix.

30
00:04:37,349 --> 00:04:49,445
The mirror entries are equal. Row 1, column 2 and row 2, column 1 are both 0.4. So H is symmetric. Type A uses this fact in step 4.

31
00:04:49,895 --> 00:04:58,193
Fact 2: H is idempotent. That means H times H equals H. Pause the video and try it. Write H twice.

32
00:04:58,643 --> 00:05:04,787
Group the two middle matrices: X transpose X inverse, times X transpose X.

33
00:05:05,237 --> 00:05:08,437
An inverse times its matrix is the identity.

34
00:05:08,887 --> 00:05:16,269
X times I is X. What is left is X, X transpose X inverse, X transpose. That is H.

35
00:05:16,719 --> 00:05:20,047
Check it on the small example. Here is H again.

36
00:05:20,497 --> 00:05:50,342
Entry 1 1 of H times H is row 1 times column 1. 0.7 times 0.7, plus 0.4 times 0.4, plus 0.1 times 0.1, plus minus 0.2 times minus 0.2. That is 0.49 plus 0.16 plus 0.01 plus 0.04, which is 0.70. This is entry 1 1 of H.

37
00:05:50,792 --> 00:05:59,155
So H times H equals H. In the picture of the last slide: to project twice is the same as to project once.

38
00:05:59,605 --> 00:06:08,949
Facts 3 and 4 are about I minus H. Pause the video and try them. The transpose of a difference is the difference of the transposes.

39
00:06:09,399 --> 00:06:22,412
The identity is symmetric, and H is symmetric by fact 1. So I minus H is symmetric. In the small example, the mirror entries of I minus H are both minus 0.4.

40
00:06:22,862 --> 00:06:31,545
Now multiply I minus H by itself. Multiply out: I I, minus I H, minus H I, plus H H.

41
00:06:31,995 --> 00:06:41,488
I times H and H times I are both H. H times H is H, by fact 2. So we get I, minus 2 H, plus H.

42
00:06:41,938 --> 00:07:05,661
Minus 2 H plus H is minus H. So I minus H, times itself, is I minus H. In the example, row 1 times column 1 is 0.09 plus 0.16 plus 0.01 plus 0.04, which is 0.30. That is entry 1 1 of I minus H.

43
00:07:06,111 --> 00:07:12,297
These are the four facts about the hat matrix. Type A uses them in step 4, to simplify.

44
00:07:12,747 --> 00:07:24,886
Section 2. Question type A asks for the mean and the variance of a fitted value, a residual, or an estimate. Lecture 8 does it on slides 27 to 41.

45
00:07:25,336 --> 00:07:35,000
Method card A has four steps. Step 1: write the quantity as a constant matrix A times a random vector, y or beta hat.

46
00:07:35,450 --> 00:07:45,605
Step 2: the mean. A constant matrix comes out of the expectation. Then put in the mean of y, X beta, or the mean of beta hat, beta.

47
00:07:46,055 --> 00:08:00,924
Step 3: the variance. The variance of A y is A, times the variance of y, times A transpose. The picture shows the sizes. Put in sigma squared I, or sigma squared X transpose X inverse.

48
00:08:01,374 --> 00:08:09,843
Step 4: simplify with X transpose X inverse times X transpose X equals I, and with the facts about H.

49
00:08:10,293 --> 00:08:13,195
Now we use the card for the fitted values.

50
00:08:13,645 --> 00:08:26,679
Now a worked problem of type A, from slides 27 to 29. Pause the video and try it. Step 1 of card A: y hat equals H y. So the constant matrix is H.

51
00:08:27,129 --> 00:08:34,532
Step 2: the mean. H is a constant, so it comes out. The mean of y hat is H times the mean of y.

52
00:08:34,982 --> 00:08:38,225
Put in the mean of y, X beta, and write out H.

53
00:08:38,675 --> 00:08:55,187
Step 4: simplify. X transpose X inverse, times X transpose X, is I. So the mean of y hat is X beta. In the picture, X beta is in the plane, so H does not change it. The fitted values are unbiased.

54
00:08:55,637 --> 00:09:05,002
Step 3: the variance. H, times the variance of y, times H transpose. The picture shows three n by n matrices.

55
00:09:05,452 --> 00:09:11,127
The variance of y is sigma squared I. H transpose is H, by fact 1.

56
00:09:11,577 --> 00:09:22,414
Step 4: sigma squared is a number, so it moves to the front. H times H is H, by fact 2. So the variance of y hat is sigma squared H.

57
00:09:22,864 --> 00:09:28,560
The results: the mean of y hat is X beta, and its variance is sigma squared H.

58
00:09:29,010 --> 00:09:38,909
The same card for the residuals, slides 30 to 32. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: e equals I minus H, times y.

59
00:09:39,359 --> 00:09:43,540
Step 2: I minus H is constant, so it comes out of the mean.

60
00:09:43,990 --> 00:09:47,574
Write out H, and put in the mean of y, X beta.

61
00:09:48,024 --> 00:09:54,872
Multiply out: X beta, minus X, X transpose X inverse, X transpose X, beta.

62
00:09:55,322 --> 00:10:09,061
Step 4: X transpose X inverse times X transpose X is I. So we get X beta minus X beta, which is the zero vector. In the picture, H does not change X beta.

63
00:10:09,511 --> 00:10:16,657
Step 3: the variance. I minus H, times the variance of y, times I minus H transpose.

64
00:10:17,107 --> 00:10:22,142
The variance of y is sigma squared I. Sigma squared moves to the front.

65
00:10:22,592 --> 00:10:27,584
Step 4: by fact 3, I minus H transpose is I minus H.

66
00:10:28,034 --> 00:10:37,399
By fact 4, I minus H times I minus H is I minus H. So the variance of e is sigma squared times I minus H.

67
00:10:37,849 --> 00:10:44,121
The results: the residuals have mean zero, and variance sigma squared times I minus H.

68
00:10:44,571 --> 00:11:02,662
One observation uses one diagonal entry. Entry i i of sigma squared H is the variance of one fitted value: sigma squared times h i i. The green bars show h i i for the 46 patients of the satisfaction data.

69
00:11:03,112 --> 00:11:17,171
The diagonal entry of I minus H is 1 minus h i i. So the variance of one residual is sigma squared times 1 minus h i i. The orange parts show it. Each bar adds to 1.

70
00:11:17,621 --> 00:11:34,986
Here h i i is between 0.027 and 0.186. So each residual has a smaller variance than the true error. Also, the residuals are not independent, because I minus H is not diagonal.

71
00:11:35,436 --> 00:11:57,644
Section 3. Now pick any covariate values, and put them in a row vector x 0. Its first entry is 1, for the intercept. The mean response is mu 0 equals x 0 beta. We estimate it with mu 0 hat equals x 0 beta hat. This is step 1 of card A, with beta hat as the random vector.

72
00:11:58,094 --> 00:12:02,147
Step 2: x 0 is a constant, so it comes out of the mean.

73
00:12:02,597 --> 00:12:11,813
The mean of beta hat is beta. So the mean of mu 0 hat is x 0 beta, which is mu 0. The estimate is unbiased.

74
00:12:12,263 --> 00:12:27,431
Step 3: the variance is x 0, times the variance of beta hat, times x 0 transpose. The sizes are 1 by p plus 1, p plus 1 by p plus 1, and p plus 1 by 1.

75
00:12:27,881 --> 00:12:33,513
Put in the variance of beta hat: sigma squared times X transpose X inverse.

76
00:12:33,963 --> 00:12:41,622
Step 4: sigma squared is a number, so it moves to the front. The result is a 1 by 1 matrix: one number.

77
00:12:42,072 --> 00:12:55,491
The results: mu 0 hat is unbiased, and its variance is sigma squared times x 0, X transpose X inverse, x 0 transpose. Type B uses this variance.

78
00:12:55,941 --> 00:13:08,015
Slide 38 builds the interval. mu 0 hat is a linear function of the normal vector beta hat, so it is normal. Its mean and variance come from the last slide.

79
00:13:08,465 --> 00:13:16,316
Subtract the mean and divide by the standard deviation. The result is standard normal. The gray curve shows it.

80
00:13:16,766 --> 00:13:32,873
Sigma is unknown. Replace it with sigma hat. By the same logic as for one coefficient, the result has a t distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. For the satisfaction data, that is 42.

81
00:13:33,323 --> 00:13:42,048
The middle area between minus t and t is 1 minus alpha. Here t is the 1 minus alpha over 2 quantile.

82
00:13:42,498 --> 00:13:57,367
Solve the two inequalities for mu 0. The interval is mu 0 hat, plus or minus t, times sigma hat, times the square root of x 0, X transpose X inverse, x 0 transpose.

83
00:13:57,817 --> 00:14:02,617
This is the confidence interval for a mean response. Type B uses it.

84
00:14:03,067 --> 00:14:13,307
Question type B asks for a confidence interval for a mean response, or a prediction interval for a new response. Method card B has six steps.

85
00:14:13,757 --> 00:14:21,864
Step 1: write the row vector x 0. The first entry is 1, for the intercept. Then the covariate values.

86
00:14:22,314 --> 00:14:29,567
Step 2: the estimate is x 0 times beta hat. A prediction for a new response is the same number.

87
00:14:30,017 --> 00:14:38,807
Step 3: compute h, the number x 0, X transpose X inverse, x 0 transpose. In the course, R computes it.

88
00:14:39,257 --> 00:14:55,171
Step 4: the standard error. For a confidence interval, it is sigma hat times the square root of h. For a prediction interval, it is sigma hat times the square root of 1 plus h. The prediction part comes later in this video.

89
00:14:55,621 --> 00:15:03,429
Step 5: the t quantile, 1 minus alpha over 2, with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

90
00:15:03,879 --> 00:15:09,810
Step 6: the interval is the estimate, plus or minus t times the standard error.

91
00:15:10,260 --> 00:15:29,481
Now a worked problem of type B, from slide 46. Pause the video and try it. The model is satisfaction on age, severity and stress, with 46 patients. Find a 95 percent confidence interval for the mean satisfaction at these covariate values.

92
00:15:29,931 --> 00:15:42,219
Step 1 of card B: the row vector x 0. It is 1, then age 38.391, severity 51.5, and stress 2.287.

93
00:15:42,669 --> 00:15:52,653
Step 2: x 0 times beta hat. Multiply each entry of x 0 by its coefficient. The coefficients are in the given box.

94
00:15:53,103 --> 00:16:13,093
The products are minus 43.828, minus 22.763, and minus 30.806. Add them to 158.491. mu 0 hat is 61.094. The dot on the number line shows it.

95
00:16:13,543 --> 00:16:23,228
Step 3: h. The computer gives x 0, X transpose X inverse, x 0 transpose: 0.02445.

96
00:16:23,678 --> 00:16:42,515
Step 4: a confidence interval uses the square root of h. The square root of 0.02445 is 0.1564. Times sigma hat, 10.058, gives a standard error of 1.573.

97
00:16:42,965 --> 00:16:54,528
Step 5: alpha is 0.05. The degrees of freedom are 46 minus 4, which is 42. The t quantile is 2.018.

98
00:16:54,978 --> 00:17:09,229
Step 6: 2.018 times 1.573 is 3.174. So the interval is 61.094 plus or minus 3.174.

99
00:17:09,679 --> 00:17:19,087
The interval is 57.92 to 64.27. This matches row 3 of the table on slide 46.

100
00:17:19,537 --> 00:17:32,422
Section 4. Now we predict one new response, not its mean. A new observation with covariate row x new is y new equals x new beta, plus its own error, epsilon new.

101
00:17:32,872 --> 00:17:39,336
The prediction is y new hat, equals x new times beta hat. This is step 1 of card A.

102
00:17:39,786 --> 00:17:43,498
Step 2: the mean of y new hat is x new times beta.

103
00:17:43,948 --> 00:17:54,572
Step 3: the variance is x new, times the variance of beta hat, times x new transpose. The sizes are the same as for the mean response.

104
00:17:55,022 --> 00:18:02,489
Step 4: the variance is sigma squared times x new, X transpose X inverse, x new transpose.

105
00:18:02,939 --> 00:18:13,435
So y new hat is the same number as mu 0 hat. But the target is different. y new is random, with its own error. So its interval must be wider.

106
00:18:13,885 --> 00:18:30,354
Slide 42 says: y new and y new hat are independent and normal. Why? y new hat is a function of the old data y only. The green curve is its error, with standard deviation 5.9 at severity 62.

107
00:18:30,804 --> 00:18:45,865
y new depends on epsilon new. The new error is independent of the old errors. So y new and y new hat are independent. The orange curve is the new error, with standard deviation sigma, here 10.1.

108
00:18:46,315 --> 00:18:53,718
The mean of the difference y new minus y new hat is x new beta minus x new beta, which is 0.

109
00:18:54,168 --> 00:18:57,389
For independent variables, the variances add.

110
00:18:57,839 --> 00:19:19,279
The variance of y new is the variance of epsilon new: sigma squared. So the variance of the difference is sigma squared, plus sigma squared times x new, X transpose X inverse, x new transpose. The purple curve is the sum. Its standard deviation is 11.7, wider than both.

111
00:19:19,729 --> 00:19:29,884
The difference is normal. Divide by its standard deviation. Sigma squared is a common factor, so the root has 1 plus the quadratic form.

112
00:19:30,334 --> 00:19:38,270
Replace sigma with sigma hat. The result has a t distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

113
00:19:38,720 --> 00:19:47,979
Solve for y new, as for the confidence interval. The prediction interval has the square root of 1 plus h. The 1 is the new error.

114
00:19:48,429 --> 00:19:55,021
This is the prediction interval. In card B, step 4 uses the square root of 1 plus h.

115
00:19:55,471 --> 00:20:12,068
A worked problem of type B with a prediction interval, from slide 47. Pause the video and try it. Find a 95 percent prediction interval for one new patient at severity 62. Then compare it with the confidence interval.

116
00:20:12,518 --> 00:20:22,203
Step 1 of card B: x new is 1, then age 38.391, severity 62, and stress 2.287.

117
00:20:22,653 --> 00:20:40,531
Step 2: the age and stress terms are the same as before. The severity term is minus 0.4420 times 62, which is minus 27.404. The prediction is 56.453.

118
00:20:40,981 --> 00:20:54,229
Step 3: the computer gives h equals 0.34174. It is larger than at severity 51.5, because 62 is far from the mean severity.

119
00:20:54,679 --> 00:21:10,892
Step 4: a prediction interval uses the square root of 1 plus h. The square root of 1.34174 is 1.1583. Times 10.058 gives 11.651.

120
00:21:11,342 --> 00:21:18,915
Step 5: the t quantile with 42 degrees of freedom is 2.018, as before.

121
00:21:19,365 --> 00:21:34,768
Step 6: 2.018 times 11.651 is 23.512. So the interval is 56.453 plus or minus 23.512.

122
00:21:35,218 --> 00:21:46,183
The prediction interval is 32.94 to 79.97. This matches row 5 on slide 47. The purple bar shows it.

123
00:21:46,633 --> 00:22:11,423
For the confidence interval, step 4 uses the square root of h, 0.5846. The standard error is 5.880, and the half-width is 11.866. The interval is 44.59 to 68.32, the green bar. The prediction interval is about twice as wide, because of the 1 for the new error.

124
00:22:11,873 --> 00:22:30,582
To recap. The hat matrix H maps y to the fitted values, and I minus H maps y to the residuals. Both are symmetric and idempotent. Card A gives means and variances: write A y, take A out of the mean, use the sandwich for the variance, and simplify.

125
00:22:31,032 --> 00:22:39,971
Card B builds an interval at new covariates: x 0, the estimate, h, the standard error, the t quantile, and the interval.

126
00:22:40,421 --> 00:22:56,399
The prediction interval has the same center as the confidence interval, but an extra 1 under the root, for the new error. At severity 62 it is about twice as wide. Video 17 b continues with more question types.
